Cyanobacteria are small microbes or bacteria that live in nearly every habitat on land and in the water. They have existed for billions of years as essential components of freshwater ecosystems and form the foundation of most aquatic food chains. When these organisms produce dangerous toxins, they are commonly referred to as harmful algal blooms or HABs.

When environmental conditions favor the growth of HABs and algae, such as warm temperatures and low or stagnant water flows, and excessive nutrient inputs, they can multiply rapidly creating nuisance blooms. Some HABs can produce toxins that can harm pets, wildlife, or people. Not all HABs produce toxins, but those that do can cause a multitude of health issues and even death. There are two types of HABs known as planktonic (HABs suspended in the water column) or benthic (HAB that grow attached to the bottom).

In the Lake Tahoe Basin, harmful algal blooms are most common during the warm weather months between late May through October, but they can occur all year.

The first known harmful algal bloom (HAB) occurred in the Lake Tahoe Keys in August 2017. The level of measured cyanotoxins was below any health concern, but the area was posted to warn the public of the potential danger and to protect pets. The Lahontan Water Board monitors the California side of the Lake Tahoe Basin for HAB issues and responds to reports of possible HABs by sampling the lake and posting results as appropriate. For HABs on the Nevada side of the Basin, the Nevada Department of Environmental Protection (NDEP) is the lead agency.

Read about the Water Board''s HAB response on the California side of the Lake Tahoe Basin.

Read about NDEP''s HAB response on the Nevada side of the Lake Tahoe Basin.

For information about the most recent HAB advisories in California (including the California side of Lake Tahoe), visit this web map.', N'On the California side of the lake, water samples are collected and tested for the presence of HABs and their associated toxins at various locations. Water Board staff respond to new bloom reports, and also administer a pre-holiday assessment monitoring program at several California beaches. HAB monitoring associated with pre-holiday assessments is intended to collect information to inform the public in California about the safety of recreational beaches during high use holiday weekends including Memorial Day, Fourth of July, and Labor Day.', 1, N'context', NULL, NULL);

To download all of the water clarity data on this page please see Tahoe Open Data.', 1, N'charts', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Lake clarity","sub":"Average Secchi depth in meters, a measure of how deep a white disk stays visible.","layer":14,"x":"year","y":[{"field":"annual_average","name":"Annual"},{"field":"winter_average","name":"Winter"},{"field":"summer_average","name":"Summer"}],"yLabel":"Secchi depth (m)","span":"span-12"},{"title":"Primary productivity","sub":"Annual algal growth in the lake.","layer":75,"x":"Year","y":"Primary_Productivity","yName":"Primary productivity","yLabel":"Primary productivity"},{"title":"Mid-lake dissolved nitrogen","sub":"Annual value at the mid-lake station.","layer":71,"x":"Year","y":"Value","yName":"Dissolved nitrogen","yLabel":"Dissolved nitrogen"},{"title":"Secchi depth readings","sub":"Every Secchi depth reading, in meters, by sample date.","layer":13,"x":"date","xType":"date","xLabel":"Sample date","y":"secchi_average_measurement","yName":"Secchi depth","yLabel":"Secchi depth (m)","zero":false,"span":"span-12"}],"sitesLayer":{"layer":12,"name":"site_name","year":"year_of_count"},"sitesPerYear":{"layer":12,"year":"year_of_count","site":"site_name"}}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Average Secchi Disk Transparency","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/111"},{"Dashboard":"Climate Resilience Dashboard","Label":"Lake Conditions","Url":"https://climate.laketahoeinfo.org/lake-conditions"},{"Dashboard":"Lake Clarity Tracker","Label":"Water Clarity","Url":"https://clarity.laketahoeinfo.org/results/water-clarity"}]');

The RSWMP Data Management System (DMS) was created for Tahoe RCD by the Desert Research Institute (DRI) and Geosyntec. The DMS retrieves and analyzes stormwater monitoring data to evaluate effectiveness of regional stormwater management strategies. That monitoring data is automatically retrieved from the DMS and displayed in LT Info. This page can be used to explore and understand the RSWMP monitoring program and select monitoring data.

Funding for RSWMP implementation, DMS development, and display on LT Info was provided by the California State Water Resources Control Board.', N'Monitoring is guided by the RSWMP Framework and Implementation Guidance document. During water year 2014 five catchments were monitored for continuous flow and turbidity and sampled for water quality at eleven monitoring stations: the outfalls of the five selected catchments, and the inflows to and outflows from selected BMPs located in three of those catchments. Three additional catchment outfalls were monitored in water year 2015. The catchments were chosen because of their direct hydrologic connectivity to Lake Tahoe, diversity of urban land uses, range of sizes, and a reasonably equitable distribution among the participating jurisdictions. BMP effectiveness sites were selected because of their potential efficacy in treating storm water runoff characteristic of the Lake Tahoe Basin, and the broad interest in, and lack of conclusive data regarding the efficiency of the selected BMPs in reducing runoff volumes and pollutant loads.', 1, N'context', NULL, N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Nitrogen Concentration (Surface Runoff)","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/141"},{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Phosphorus Concentration (Surface Runoff)","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/142"},{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Reduce Fine Sediment Particle Load","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/176"},{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Reduce Loading of Dissolved Phosphorus","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/180"},{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Reduce Total Annual Phosphorus Load","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/177"},{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Suspended Sediment Concentration (Surface Runoff)","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/144"},{"Dashboard":"Climate Resilience Dashboard","Label":"Watershed Resilience","Url":"https://climate.laketahoeinfo.org/watershed-resilience"},{"Dashboard":"Lake Clarity Tracker","Label":"Results Overview","Url":"https://clarity.laketahoeinfo.org/results/overview"}]');

Phosphorus is a nutrient important to the growth and reproduction of plants and is considered a pollutant of concern in the Lake Tahoe Region (Lahontan and NDEP, 2010b)

Threshold standard: Reduce total annual phosphorus load to achieve long-term pelagic water quality standards (WQ1 and WQ2) and littoral quality standards (WQ5 and WQ6).', NULL, 0, N'charts', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Phosphorus load reduction achieved","sub":"Pounds of phosphorus kept out of Lake Tahoe each year by projects that TMDL implementors credit through the Lake Clarity Crediting Program, 2023 Threshold Evaluation.","json":"data/threshold-evaluation/phosphorus-load-reduction.json","sourceHref":"https://trpa-agency.github.io/ThresholdEvaluation/2023/WaterQuality/Chart/Final/Phosphorous_Load_Reduction.html","sourceLabel":"2023 Threshold Evaluation","x":"Year","xLabel":"Year","y":"Pounds per year","yName":"Load reduction","yLabel":"Pounds per year","type":"bar","span":"span-12"}]}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Reduce Total Annual Phosphorus Load","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/177"},{"Dashboard":"Lake Clarity Tracker","Label":"Results Overview","Url":"https://clarity.laketahoeinfo.org/results/overview"}]');

Nitrogen is a nutrient important to the growth and reproduction of plants, and it is considered a pollutant of concern in the Lake Tahoe Basin. The TMDL establishes targets for nitrogen reduction to restore the clarity of the lake.

Threshold standard: Reduce total annual nitrogen load to achieve long-term pelagic water quality standards (WQ1 and WQ2) and littoral quality standards (WQ5 and WQ6).', NULL, 0, N'charts', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Nitrogen load reduction achieved","sub":"Pounds of nitrogen kept out of Lake Tahoe each year by projects that TMDL implementors credit through the Lake Clarity Crediting Program, 2023 Threshold Evaluation.","json":"data/threshold-evaluation/nitrogen-load-reduction.json","sourceHref":"https://trpa-agency.github.io/ThresholdEvaluation/2023/WaterQuality/Chart/Final/Nitrogen_Load_Reduction.html","sourceLabel":"2023 Threshold Evaluation","x":"Year","xLabel":"Year","y":"Pounds per year","yName":"Load reduction","yLabel":"Pounds per year","type":"bar","span":"span-12"}]}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Reduce Total Annual Nitrogen Load","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/178"},{"Dashboard":"Lake Clarity Tracker","Label":"Results Overview","Url":"https://clarity.laketahoeinfo.org/results/overview"}]');

Runoff from roads and other urban land uses is the single largest source of fine sediment particles (FSP) impacting Lake Tahoe’s clarity, accounting for more than 70 percent of the FSP load to the lake. TMDL research also found that urban stormwater also provides the greatest opportunity to control FSP pollution. Consequently, restoring Lake Tahoe’s clarity hinges on achieving FSP load reductions in urban stormwater.

Threshold standard: Decrease total annual suspended sediment load to achieve littoral turbidity standards (WQ3 and WQ4).', N'Decrease total annual suspended sediment load to achieve littoral turbidity standards (WQ3 and WQ4).', 0, N'charts', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Fine sediment load reduction achieved","sub":"Pounds of fine sediment kept out of Lake Tahoe each year by projects that TMDL implementors credit through the Lake Clarity Crediting Program, 2023 Threshold Evaluation.","json":"data/threshold-evaluation/fine-sediment-load-reduction.json","sourceHref":"https://trpa-agency.github.io/ThresholdEvaluation/2023/WaterQuality/Chart/Final/Fine_Sediment_Load_Reduction.html","sourceLabel":"2023 Threshold Evaluation","x":"Year","xLabel":"Year","y":"Pounds per year","yName":"Load reduction","yLabel":"Pounds per year","type":"bar","span":"span-12"}]}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Decrease Total Annual Suspended Sediment Load","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/179"},{"Dashboard":"Lake Clarity Tracker","Label":"Results Overview","Url":"https://clarity.laketahoeinfo.org/results/overview"}]');

Recommended CNEL levels for transportation corridors were developed as part of the 2012 Regional Plan Update. CNEL thresholds will be attained upon implementation of the following policy:

Transmission of Noise from the Transportation Corridors Shall be Reduced. The noise associated with the transportation corridors can be decreased by reducing the number of trips and by installing mitigation measures. Trip reduction will be accomplished by the transit improvements identified in the Transportation Element (of the Regional Plan). Ordinances will establish specific site design criteria for projects to help reduce the transmission of noise from the transportation corridors. The design criteria will also be incorporated into the water quality and transportation improvement programs. The mitigation measures may include setbacks, earth berms, and barriers.

The data from this monitoring program informs the need for specific design criteria and mitigations measures as written in section 68.8.3 in the TRPA Code of Ordinances.', N'TRPA uses a Community Noise Equivalent Level (CNEL) meaure to assess whether noise levels are being exceeded in highway corridors. The CNEL averages decible (dB) levels over a 24 hour period, with excess noise late at night and early in the morning being weighted greater due to humans and wildlife being more sensitive to noise at these times.The highest 24-hour CNEL measured is used to assess noise levels. TRPA regularly monitors noise along highway corridors at 30 locations around the Tahoe Basin, with monitors located 300 feet from the edge of the highway. Noise monitors are deployed for 1-2 weeks during the peak highway useage period, which is generally July 4th through Labor Day. Each noise monitoring location is re-visited once every four years. Noise monitors are calibrated to industry standards to ensure accuracy.

The following CNEL noise limits are in place for highways in the Tahoe Basin:

Highway 207 (Kingsbury Grade) = 55 dB (blue)

Highway 431 (Mt Rose Highway) = 55 dB (yellow)

Highway 89 = 55 dB (orange)

Highway 28 = 55 dB (red)

Highway 50 = 65 dB (brown)

To download all of the highway noise data on this page please see Tahoe Open Data.', 1, N'map', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Highway noise by year","sub":"Average community noise equivalent level (CNEL) at highway monitoring sites, in decibels.","layer":11,"x":"YEAR_OF_COUNT","y":"CNEL_AVG_VALUE","stat":"avg","yLabel":"Average CNEL (dB)","seriesBy":"HIGHWAY","seriesLabel":"Highway","seriesPrefix":"Highway ","type":"bar","span":"span-12"}],"sitesLayer":{"layer":11,"name":"SITE_NAME","year":"YEAR_OF_COUNT"},"sitesPerYear":{"layer":11,"year":"YEAR_OF_COUNT","site":"SITE_NAME"},"siteProfile":{"record":{"year":"YEAR_OF_COUNT","value":"CNEL_AVG_VALUE","label":"Average CNEL","unit":"dBA","decimals":1,"noun":["year monitored","years monitored"],"standard":{"field":"CNEL_LIMIT","label":"limit"},"zero":false},"facts":[{"label":"Latest maximum","field":"CNEL_MAX_VALUE","suffix":" dBA","decimals":1},{"label":"From road edge","field":"DISTANCE_FROM_EDGE","suffix":" ft"}],"link":"LTINFO_HYPERLINK"}}', NULL);

Data found in this monitoring dashboard informs evaluation of TRPA thresholds for allowable community noise levels and evaluation of initial environmental checklists as part of the TRPA project review process written in section 68.8.1 in the TRPA Code of Ordinances.', N'TRPA uses a Community Noise Equivalent Level (CNEL) meaure to assess whether noise levels are being exceeded in Plan Areas. The CNEL averages decible levels over a 24 hour period, with excess noise late at night and early in the morning being weighted greater due to humans and wildlife being more sensitive to noise at these times. Noise monitors are generally put out in Plan Areas for 1-2 weeks during peak noise periods (generally summer). The average 24-hour CNEL measured is used to assess noise levels. Construction noise or other unusual noise events are excluded from the data. Noise monitors are calibrated and tested to noise industry standards to ensure proper measurements. TRPA monitors each of the 9 Plan Area types per year.

In general, the following CNEL noise levels are allowed in different land use categroies:

Wilderness and roadless areas = 45 dB

Low density residential areas = 50 dB

Rural outdoor recreation areas = 50 dB

High density residential areas = 55 dB

Urban outdoor recreation areas = 55 dB

Commercial areas = 60 dB

Hotel / motel areas = 60 dB

Industrial areas = 65 dB

