mobility with the Tahoe Region. Published traffic volumes are counted annually within the Lake Tahoe Region by both the California Department of Transportation (Caltrans) the Nevada Department of Transportation (NDOT) and local governmental jurisdictions. In addition to modeling compliance with

the 1981 VMT Threshold, TRPA staff utilized the 1981 base year VMT estimate, and the corresponding traffic count stations that produce annual traffic counts to analyze increases or decreases in VMT.', 0, N'charts', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Vehicle miles traveled","sub":"Estimated vehicle miles traveled per day in the Tahoe Region. The threshold is 10 percent below the 1981 base year.","layer":92,"x":"Year","y":"VMT","yName":"VMT","yLabel":"Vehicle miles per day","standard":{"value":1309316,"label":"Threshold"},"zero":false,"span":"span-12"}]}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Climate Resilience Dashboard","Label":"Transportation","Url":"https://climate.laketahoeinfo.org/transportation"}]');

More than a century ago, natural fire kept a mosaic of early, mid, and late seral stands across the Tahoe Basin, with a balance of open and closed canopies. Clear-cutting in the Comstock era, followed by more than 100 years of fire suppression and past management, left many stands dense, uniform, and heavy with fuel. Dense, middle-aged forest is overrepresented today.

The Seral Stage and Canopy Cover threshold standard calls for at least 75 percent of forested acres to fall within desired ranges for seral stage and canopy cover. The ranges are set for each of three forest types, Jeffrey pine, Sierra mixed conifer, and red fir, and reflect conditions that support resilience, habitat diversity, and fire-adapted forest structure.

Meeting the ranges takes both active management, such as thinning and beneficial fire to open up canopies, and protection that lets existing stands mature into late seral forest.', N'Seral stage and canopy cover are mapped across the conifer forest from the Sierra Nevada Regional Resource Kit, 30-meter remote sensing products, in the 2023 assessment as published to the TRPA Vegetation service. Seral stage follows quadratic mean diameter: early under 5 inches, mid 5 to 25 inches, and late over 25 inches. Closed canopy is 40 percent cover or more in Jeffrey pine, and 50 percent or more in Sierra mixed conifer and red fir.

Each forest type''s acres are compared with its desired share in five classes: early seral, mid seral open, mid seral closed, late seral open, and late seral closed.

Jeffrey pine: early 5 to 15 percent, mid open 25 to 30 percent, mid closed 5 to 10 percent, late open 40 to 50 percent, and late closed 5 to 10 percent.

Sierra mixed conifer: early 10 to 20 percent, mid open 15 to 20 percent, mid closed 5 to 15 percent, late open 25 to 35 percent, and late closed 15 to 25 percent.

Red fir: early 10 to 20 percent, mid open 15 to 25 percent, mid closed 15 to 25 percent, late open 30 to 40 percent, and late closed 20 to 30 percent.', 0, N'charts', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Conifer forest acres by seral stage and canopy cover","sub":"Acres of conifer forest in each seral stage and canopy class, 2023 assessment.","service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Vegetation/FeatureServer","layer":3,"x":"Composition","xType":"category","xLabel":"Seral stage and canopy","xOrder":["Early Seral","Mid Seral Open Canopy","Mid Seral Closed Canopy","Late Seral Open Canopy","Late Seral Closed Canopy"],"xNames":{"Early Seral":"Early","Mid Seral Open Canopy":"Mid, open canopy","Mid Seral Closed Canopy":"Mid, closed canopy","Late Seral Open Canopy":"Late, open canopy","Late Seral Closed Canopy":"Late, closed canopy"},"y":"Acres","stat":"sum","yName":"Acres","yLabel":"Acres","type":"bar","span":"span-12"}]}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Seral Stage & Canopy Cover","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/190"}]');

After a century of fire suppression, Tahoe''s forests are far denser than they were historically. Crowded trees compete for water, nutrients, and sunlight, and stressed trees are more vulnerable to drought, insects, and disease. Recent droughts have raised mortality: the USDA Forest Service aerial detection survey recorded more than 1.7 million dead trees across 66,000 acres of the Tahoe Basin in 2023.

The Stand Density threshold standard calls for at least 50 percent of forested area to fall within recommended ranges of trees per acre and basal area for its forest type and seral stage. Thinning overcrowded stands reduces competition among trees and improves their resilience to drought and wildfire.', N'Trees per acre and basal area are mapped across the conifer forest from the Sierra Nevada Regional Resource Kit, 30-meter remote sensing products, in the 2023 assessment as published to the TRPA Vegetation service, and tested together against the recommended maximum for each forest type and seral stage. An acre at or below both values is within range.

Jeffrey pine: early seral 200 trees and 30 square feet of basal area per acre, mid seral 70 trees and 80 square feet, and late seral 55 trees and 100 square feet.

Sierra mixed conifer: early seral 300 trees and 40 square feet, mid seral 90 trees and 130 square feet, and late seral 75 trees and 180 square feet.

Red fir: early seral 300 trees and 50 square feet, mid seral 100 trees and 175 square feet, and late seral 80 trees and 250 square feet.', 0, N'charts', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Conifer forest acres within the recommended density","sub":"Acres at or below the recommended trees per acre and basal area for their forest type and seral stage, and acres over it, 2023 assessment.","service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Vegetation/FeatureServer","layer":4,"x":"Status","xType":"category","xLabel":"Stand density","xOrder":["Under Target","Over Target"],"xNames":{"Under Target":"Within recommended density","Over Target":"Over recommended density"},"y":"Acres","stat":"sum","yName":"Acres","yLabel":"Acres","type":"bar","span":"span-12"}]}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Stand Density","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/189"}]');

Comstock-era logging cut most of the Basin''s large trees in the late 1800s. The trees that escaped it, and the forest that has grown since, set how much old forest structure the Basin has today. Counting large trees over time shows whether that structure is recovering or being lost to drought, insects, and severe fire.', N'Individual trees were mapped across the Basin from airborne LiDAR flown in 2010, with each tree''s diameter estimated from its LiDAR measurements. The layer holds the 84,373 trees estimated at more than 36 inches.

The map counts the trees in hexagons of 100 acres each. The count will be repeated with the next Basin-wide LiDAR flight, so the change in large trees can be measured.', 0, N'map', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Large trees by diameter","sub":"Trees more than 36 inches in diameter at breast height, by estimated diameter, 2010 LiDAR.","json":"data/large-trees/trees-by-diameter.json","sourceHref":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Large_Trees_Reference/FeatureServer/0","sourceLabel":"TRPA Large_Trees_Reference map service, layer 0","x":"Diameter","xType":"category","xLabel":"Diameter at breast height","y":"Trees","yName":"Trees","yLabel":"Trees","type":"bar","span":"span-12"}],"mapLayer":{"url":"data/large-trees/large-tree-hexagons.geojson","sourceHref":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Large_Trees_Reference/FeatureServer/0","sourceLabel":"TRPA Large_Trees_Reference map service, layer 0, counted in 100-acre hexagons","layer":0,"title":"Large trees per 100 acres","unit":"hexagon","unitPlural":"Hexagons","name":"Cell","countAsSites":false,"list":false,"colorBy":"Trees","colorByLabel":"Large trees per 100 acres","breaks":[{"max":10,"label":"1 to 10","color":"var(--mon-turbid-2)"},{"max":50,"label":"11 to 50","color":"var(--mon-turbid-3)"},{"max":100,"label":"51 to 100","color":"var(--mon-turbid-4)"},{"max":250,"label":"101 to 250","color":"var(--mon-turbid-5)"},{"max":100000,"label":"More than 250","color":"var(--mon-turbid-7)"}],"fields":[{"field":"Trees","label":"Large trees"}]}}', NULL);

The 2023 survey found fir engraver beetles killing California red fir across the Basin''s upper slopes, with Jeffrey pine beetle and mountain pine beetle in smaller patches. Dead trees add to the fuel that can carry severe fire, which ties tree mortality to both stand density and fire dynamics.', N'The USDA Forest Service aerial detection survey is flown over California''s forests each summer. Surveyors sketch each area of recent damage from the air and record the host tree, the agent that killed it, the share of trees affected, and an estimate of trees killed.

This page reads the 2023 survey: the damage areas within or touching the TRPA boundary. Areas that cross the boundary are counted whole, so the totals run higher than the Basin alone. Each year''s survey added to the service builds the trend.', 0, N'map', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Acres with tree mortality by host tree","sub":"Acres of the 2023 damage areas by the tree species killed.","service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Vegetation/FeatureServer","layer":2,"where":"WITHIN_TRPA_BNDY = 1","x":"HOST","xType":"category","xLabel":"Host tree","xNames":{"California red fir - 20":"California red fir","Jeffrey pine - 116":"Jeffrey pine","whitebark pine - 101":"Whitebark pine","lodgepole pine - 108":"Lodgepole pine","white fir - 15":"White fir"},"y":"ACRES","stat":"sum","yName":"Acres","yLabel":"Acres","type":"bar","orientation":"h","sort":"desc"},{"title":"Trees killed by agent","sub":"Estimated dead trees in the 2023 damage areas by the insect that killed them.","service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Vegetation/FeatureServer","layer":2,"where":"WITHIN_TRPA_BNDY = 1","x":"DCA","xType":"category","xLabel":"Agent","xNames":{"Fir Engraver - 11050":"Fir engraver","Jeffrey Pine Beetle - 11004":"Jeffrey pine beetle","Mountain Pine Beetle - 11006":"Mountain pine beetle"},"y":"NUMBER_OF_TREES","stat":"sum","yName":"Trees killed","yLabel":"Trees","type":"bar","orientation":"h","sort":"desc"},{"title":"Acres by share of trees affected","sub":"Acres of the 2023 damage areas by the share of trees killed in each.","service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Vegetation/FeatureServer","layer":2,"where":"WITHIN_TRPA_BNDY = 1 AND PCT_AFFECTED IS NOT NULL","x":"PCT_AFFECTED","xType":"category","xLabel":"Trees affected","xOrder":["Very Light (1-3%)","Light (4-10%)","Moderate (11-29%)","Severe (30-50%)","Very Severe (>50%)"],"xNames":{"Very Light (1-3%)":"Very light, 1 to 3 percent","Light (4-10%)":"Light, 4 to 10 percent","Moderate (11-29%)":"Moderate, 11 to 29 percent","Severe (30-50%)":"Severe, 30 to 50 percent","Very Severe (>50%)":"Very severe, more than 50 percent"},"y":"ACRES","stat":"sum","yName":"Acres","yLabel":"Acres","type":"bar","span":"span-12"}],"mapLayer":{"service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Vegetation/FeatureServer","layer":2,"where":"WITHIN_TRPA_BNDY = 1","title":"Tree mortality, 2023 aerial survey","unit":"damage area","unitPlural":"Damage areas","name":"HOST","countAsSites":false,"list":false,"colorBy":"PCT_AFFECTED","colorByLabel":"Trees affected","labels":{"Very Light (1-3%)":"Very light, 1 to 3 percent","Light (4-10%)":"Light, 4 to 10 percent","Moderate (11-29%)":"Moderate, 11 to 29 percent","Severe (30-50%)":"Severe, 30 to 50 percent","Very Severe (>50%)":"Very severe, more than 50 percent"},"colors":{"Very Light (1-3%)":"var(--mon-turbid-1)","Light (4-10%)":"var(--mon-turbid-2)","Moderate (11-29%)":"var(--mon-turbid-4)","Severe (30-50%)":"var(--mon-turbid-5)","Very Severe (>50%)":"var(--mon-turbid-7)"},"meta":[{"field":"ACRES","suffix":" acres"}],"fields":[{"field":"HOST","label":"Host tree"},{"field":"DCA","label":"Agent"},{"field":"PCT_AFFECTED","label":"Trees affected"},{"field":"NUMBER_OF_TREES","label":"Trees killed"},{"field":"ACRES","label":"Acres"}]}}', NULL);

61 percent of the Subalpine zone (greater than 8,500 feet elevation) must be in a late seral or old growth condition. The Subalpine zone will contribute 5 percent (7,600 acres) of forested lands towards VP12.

Attain and maintain a minimum percentage of 55 percent by area of forested lands within the Tahoe Region in a late seral or old growth condition, and distributed across elevation zones. Standards VP 13, VP14, and VP15 must be attained to achieve this threshold.

60 percent of the Upper Montane zone (between 7,000 and 8,500 feet elevation) must be in a late seral or old growth condition. The Upper Montane zone will contribute 30 percent (45,900 acres) of forested lands towards VP12.', N'Every five years, the Tahoe vegetation map is updated with new satellite data (if

available) and/or modeled and calibrated using field-based Forest Inventory and Analysis (FIA) data to assess the extent of different vegetation types and associated forest structure characteristics for the Region (USDA, 2009; Warbington et al., 2011). For this analysis, California Wildlife Habitat Relationship

(CWHR) vegetation types associated with large diameter trees were queried and enumerated from the most recently available vegetation map (U.S. Forest Service - Remote Sensing Lab Pacific Southwest Region: TMU_Strata_07 [published 2009]).', 0, N'charts', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Seral stage by elevation zone","sub":"Percent of undisturbed vegetation in each seral stage, across all forest and in each elevation zone, 2023 Threshold Evaluation.","json":"data/threshold-evaluation/seral-stage-by-zone.json","sourceHref":"https://trpa-agency.github.io/ThresholdEvaluation/2023/Vegetation/Chart/Final/Vegetation_SeralStage.html","sourceLabel":"2023 Threshold Evaluation","x":"Zone","xType":"category","xLabel":"Zone","xOrder":["All forest","Montane","Upper montane","Subalpine"],"y":"Percent","seriesBy":"Seral stage","seriesLabel":"Seral stage","seriesOrder":["Early Seral","Mid Seral","Late Seral","Not Classified","Not classified"],"seriesColors":{"Early Seral":"var(--mon-greens-1)","Mid Seral":"var(--mon-greens-3)","Late Seral":"var(--mon-greens-6)","Not Classified":"var(--color-content-disabled)"},"yLabel":"Percent of undisturbed vegetation","type":"stacked","span":"span-12"}],"mapLayer":{"service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Vegetation_Late_Seral/MapServer","layer":0,"title":"Late seral forest (2010)","unit":"stand","unitPlural":"Stands","name":"TRPA_VegType","list":false,"countAsSites":false,"colorBy":"SeralStage","colorByLabel":"Seral stage","colors":{"Late Seral Open":"var(--mon-greens-4)","Late Seral Closed":"var(--mon-greens-6)"},"fields":[{"field":"TRPA_VegType","label":"Vegetation type"},{"field":"SeralStage","label":"Seral stage"},{"field":"QMD","label":"Quadratic mean diameter (in.)"},{"field":"Acres","label":"Acres"}]}}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Montane Zone","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/88"},{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Sub-Alpine Zone","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/86"},{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Total Old Growth","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/85"},{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Upper Montane Zone","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/87"}]');

Relative Abundance - Of the total amount of undisturbed vegetation in the Tahoe Basin: Maintain 15-25 percent of the Yellow Pine Forest in seral stages other than mature.', N'For this evaluation, stands dominated by trees less than 10.9-inches diameter at breast height (dbh) were enumerated from the following California Wildlife Habitat Relationship (CWHR) Types (CWHR, 2011) attributed in the U.S. Forest Service - Remote Sensing Lab Pacific Southwest Region TMU_Strata_07 map layer (published 2009). Every five years, the Tahoe vegetation map is updated with new satellite data (if available) and/or modeled and calibrated using field-based forest inventory and analysis data to assess the extent of different vegetation types and associated forest structure characteristics for the Region (USDA, 2009; Warbington et al., 2011).', 0, N'charts', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Yellow pine and red fir forest by successional stage","sub":"Acres of yellow pine and red fir forest in each successional stage, from the 2010 EcObject LiDAR vegetation map. Every stage but mature counts toward this indicator.","layer":98,"where":"TRPA_VegType IN (''Yellow Pine Forest'',''Red Fir Forest'') AND Top_Succes <> ''''","x":"Top_Succes","xType":"category","xLabel":"Successional stage","y":"Acres","stat":"sum","seriesBy":"TRPA_VegType","seriesLabel":"Forest type","yLabel":"Acres","type":"bar","span":"span-12"},{"title":"Jeffrey pine and red fir dominated stands outside the mature stage","sub":"Acres where Jeffrey pine or red fir is the dominant tree, by successional stage other than mature (2010).","layer":98,"where":"REGIONAL_DOMINANCE_TYPE_1 IN (''JP'',''RF'') AND Top_Succes NOT IN (''Mature'','''')","x":"Top_Succes","xType":"category","xLabel":"Successional stage","y":"Acres","stat":"sum","seriesBy":"REGIONAL_DOMINANCE_TYPE_1","seriesLabel":"Dominant tree","seriesNames":{"JP":"Jeffrey pine","RF":"Red fir"},"yLabel":"Acres","type":"stacked"}]}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Relative Abundance of Red Fir Forest In Seral Stages Other Than Mature","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/75"},{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Relative Abundance of Yellow Pine Forest In Seral Stages Other Than Mature","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/76"}]');

the metric estimates', NULL, 0, N'charts', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Acres by vegetation type","sub":"Acres of each TRPA vegetation type in the 2010 EcObject LiDAR vegetation map of the Tahoe Basin.","layer":98,"where":"TRPA_VegType <> ''''","x":"TRPA_VegType","xType":"category","xLabel":"Vegetation type","y":"Acres","stat":"sum","yName":"Acres","yLabel":"Acres","type":"bar","orientation":"h","sort":"desc"},{"title":"Forest acres by horizontal pattern","sub":"Acres of forest in each tree spatial pattern class, from individual trees to dense clumps (2010).","layer":98,"where":"TRPA_VegType IN (''Yellow Pine Forest'',''Red Fir Forest'',''Subalpine Forest'')","x":"SpatialVar","xType":"category","xLabel":"Spatial pattern","y":"Acres","stat":"sum","seriesBy":"TRPA_VegType","seriesLabel":"Forest type","yLabel":"Acres","type":"stacked"}]}', NULL);

Vegetation is integral to many scenic and recreational amenities in the Lake Tahoe Region. Vegetation also provides many functional roles related to water cleansing, soil stabilization, wildlife habitat, nutrient catchment and release, air purification, and noise control. The focus of vegetation preservation in the Region is to restore, protect and maintain these functions and contribute to other socioeconomic attributes. Specifically, this management standard discourages the creation of large forest openings, such as clear cuts, while providing tools to allow for forest openings of up to eight acres in size to meet specific management goals such as regeneration of shade intolerant species (e.g., Jeffery and sugar pine). It also encourages the perpetuation of a diversity of tree age classes, which is important for ensuring the sustainability of the Region’s forests.

Threshold standard: Pattern - Provide for the proper juxtaposition of vegetation communities and age classes by; 1. Limiting acreage size of new forest openings to no more than eight acres', N'Has TRPA adopted appropriate policies, ordinances and/or programs to support the proper juxtaposition of vegetation communities and age classes?', 0, N'map', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Caldor Fire vegetation burn severity","sub":"Acres of the 2021 Caldor Fire in each vegetation burn severity class, the whole fire and not only the Tahoe Basin.","service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Fire/MapServer","layer":5,"where":"gridcode IN (1,2,3,4)","x":"gridcode","xType":"category","xLabel":"Burn severity","xNames":{"1":"Unburned or very low","2":"Low","3":"Moderate","4":"High"},"y":"Shape.STArea() / 4046.8564224","stat":"sum","seriesBy":"gridcode","seriesLabel":"Burn severity","seriesNames":{"1":"Unburned or very low","2":"Low","3":"Moderate","4":"High"},"seriesOrder":["1","2","3","4"],"seriesColors":{"1":"#9ebbd7","2":"#6699cd","3":"#cdcd66","4":"#895a44"},"yLabel":"Acres","type":"stacked","span":"span-12"}],"mapLayer":{"service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Fire/MapServer","layer":5,"where":"gridcode = 4","title":"Caldor Fire high severity burn (2021)","unit":"burn patch","unitPlural":"Burn patches","name":"gridcode","list":false,"countAsSites":false,"colorBy":"gridcode","colorByLabel":"Vegetation burn severity","colors":{"4":"#895a44"},"labels":{"4":"High"},"fields":[]},"overlays":[{"service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Fire/MapServer","layer":6,"title":"Fire history (LTBMU, 1967 to 2016)","name":"FIRE_NAME","geometry":"fill","color":"#b53535","where":"UNIT_ID = ''TMU''","fields":[{"field":"FIRE_NAME","label":"Fire"},{"field":"YEAR_","label":"Year"},{"field":"GIS_ACRES","label":"Acres"}]}]}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Pattern: Limit Size Of New Forest Openings","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/80"}]');

Vegetation is integral to many scenic and recreational amenities in the Lake Tahoe Region. Vegetation also provides many functional roles related to water cleansing, soil stabilization, wildlife habitat, nutrient catchment and release, air purification, and noise control. The focus of vegetation preservation in the Region is to restore, protect and maintain these functions and contribute to other socioeconomic attributes. Specifically, this management standard discourages the creation of large forest openings, such as clear cuts, while providing tools to allow for forest openings of up to eight acres in size to meet specific management goals such as regeneration of shade intolerant species (e.g., Jeffery and sugar pine). It also encourages the perpetuation of a diversity of tree age classes, which is important for ensuring the sustainability of the Region’s forests.

Threshold standard: Pattern –Provide for the proper juxtaposition of vegetation communities and age classes by; 2. Adjacent openings shall not be of the same relative age class or successional stage to avoid uniformity in stand composition and age.', N'Has TRPA adopted appropriate policies, ordinances and/or programs to support the proper juxtaposition of vegetation communities and age classes?', 0, N'context', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Seral stage by canopy class","sub":"Percent of undisturbed vegetation in each seral stage, open and closed canopy, 2023 Threshold Evaluation.","json":"data/threshold-evaluation/stand-composition.json","sourceHref":"https://trpa-agency.github.io/ThresholdEvaluation/2023/Vegetation/Chart/Final/Vegetation_Composition.html","sourceLabel":"2023 Threshold Evaluation","x":"Seral stage","xType":"category","xLabel":"Seral stage","xOrder":["Early Seral","Mid Seral","Late Seral","Not Classified","Not classified"],"xNames":{"Early Seral":"Early","Mid Seral":"Mid","Late Seral":"Late","Not classified":"Not classified"},"y":"Percent","seriesBy":"Canopy","seriesLabel":"Canopy","seriesOrder":["Open","Closed"],"seriesNames":{"Not stated":"Not classified"},"seriesColors":{"Open":"var(--mon-greens-2)","Closed":"var(--mon-greens-5)","Not stated":"var(--color-content-disabled)"},"yLabel":"Percent of undisturbed vegetation","type":"stacked","span":"span-12"}]}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Pattern: Stand Composition And Age","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/81"},{"Dashboard":"Climate Resilience Dashboard","Label":"Forest Resilience and Wildfire Risk","Url":"https://climate.laketahoeinfo.org/forest-resilience-and-wildfire-risk"}]');

Forest management activities have the potential for substantial impacts on the environment. However, the forests of Lake Tahoe are in need of active management to maintain forest health and reduce the threat of wildfire. The importance of appropriate low-impact forest management cannot be overstated, and this policy statement was intended to ensure that forest management activities comply with all TRPA Regional Plan policies and ordinances adopted to achieve multiple TRPA threshold standards.

Threshold standard: Increase plant and structural diversity of forest communities through appropriate management practices as measured by diversity indices of species richness, relative abundance, and pattern.', N'Detailed in the analysis section.', 0, N'context', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Forest fuel treatment acres by zone","sub":"Acres of forest fuel reduction treatment completed each year, by forest management zone, 2023 Threshold Evaluation.","json":"data/threshold-evaluation/forest-fuel-treatment.json","sourceHref":"https://trpa-agency.github.io/ThresholdEvaluation/2023/Vegetation/Chart/Final/ForestFuelTreatment.html","sourceLabel":"2023 Threshold Evaluation","x":"Year","y":"Acres","seriesBy":"Zone","seriesLabel":"Zone","seriesOrder":["Defense Zone","Threat Zone","General Forest","Urban Core"],"seriesColors":{"Defense Zone":"#e69800","Threat Zone":"#f5ca7a","General Forest":"#abcd66","Urban Core":"var(--color-content-disabled)"},"yLabel":"Acres","type":"stacked","span":"span-12"},{"title":"Forest fuel treatment acres","sub":"Acres of initial and maintenance forest fuel reduction treatment completed each year in the Tahoe Basin, through 2020.","layer":90,"where":"Year IS NOT NULL","x":"Year","y":[{"field":"Initial","name":"Initial"},{"field":"Maintenance","name":"Maintenance"}],"yLabel":"Acres","type":"stacked","span":"span-12"}]}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Appropriate Management Practices","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/84"}]');

Burn probability is the likelihood half of wildfire hazard; likely fire severity is the intensity half. Together they show where fire is most likely to come and how hard it would burn. Fuel treatments lower both over time.', N'Vibrant Planet''s Quantitative Wildfire Risk Assessment for the Lake Tahoe Basin models annual and 10-year burn probability with the FSim fire simulator, on fuels updated for disturbance and regrowth through the end of 2025. It replaces the 2022 CAL FIRE modeling the Region uses now, which reflects fuels through 2021.

Burn probability will be reported as the share of each forest management zone in each probability class, and repeated with each model update.', 0, N'charts', NULL, N'[{"Dashboard":"Climate Resilience Dashboard","Label":"Forest Resilience and Wildfire Risk","Url":"https://climate.laketahoeinfo.org/forest-resilience-and-wildfire-risk"}]');

Likely fire severity is the outcome a fire would most likely have in each part of the forest under hot, dry, windy weather: low, moderate, or high severity. Low-severity fire clears brush and small trees and leaves most large trees alive. High-severity fire kills most trees, and large high-severity patches leave few surviving trees to reseed the forest.

Two threshold standards read it. The WUI Defense Zone standard calls for at least 90 percent of the Defense Zone, the land right around homes and roads, to have predicted flame lengths of four feet or less, the limit at which firefighters can safely attack a fire directly. The Landscape standard limits modeled high-severity patches of 200 acres or more to less than 5 percent of the forested landscape.

In the 2021 Caldor Fire, many treated areas burned at low severity, but nearly 5,000 acres burned at high severity in large patches, closely matching what the modeling predicted.', N'Fire severity is modeled across the Basin under 90th percentile fire weather with the Pyrologix Wildfire Exposure Simulation Tool, delivered through the Sierra Nevada Regional Resource Kit. Each 30-meter cell gets its most likely severity, low, moderate, or high, summarized here by forest management zone.

Low severity stands in for flame lengths of four feet or less. Vibrant Planet''s Quantitative Wildfire Risk Assessment will replace this run with flame length at the 90th, 95th, and 97th percentile of fire weather, on fuels updated through 2025.', 0, N'charts', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Most likely fire severity by management zone","sub":"Acres of each forest management zone by most likely fire severity under 90th percentile fire weather.","service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Fire/MapServer","layer":3,"where":"ManagementZone <> ''''","x":"ManagementZone","xType":"category","xLabel":"Management zone","xOrder":["WUI Defense Zone","WUI Threat Zone","General Forest","Desolation Wilderness","Granite Chief Wilderness","Mt. Rose Wilderness"],"y":"Acres","stat":"sum","seriesBy":"MostLikelyFireSeverity","seriesLabel":"Severity","seriesNames":{"Low Severity":"Low","Moderate Severity":"Moderate","High Severity":"High"},"seriesOrder":["Low Severity","Moderate Severity","High Severity"],"seriesColors":{"Low Severity":"#6699cd","Moderate Severity":"#cdcd66","High Severity":"#895a44"},"yLabel":"Acres","type":"stacked","span":"span-12"},{"title":"WUI Defense Zone by most likely fire severity","sub":"Acres of the mapped WUI Defense Zone, urban footprint included, by most likely fire severity. Low severity stands in for flame lengths of four feet or less.","service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Fire/MapServer","layer":3,"where":"ManagementZone = ''WUI Defense Zone''","x":"MostLikelyFireSeverity","xType":"category","xLabel":"Most likely fire severity","xOrder":["Low Severity","Moderate Severity","High Severity"],"xNames":{"Low Severity":"Low","Moderate Severity":"Moderate","High Severity":"High"},"y":"Acres","stat":"sum","yName":"Acres","yLabel":"Acres","type":"bar"},{"title":"Most likely high severity fire by management zone","sub":"Acres where high severity is the most likely outcome, all patch sizes. The Landscape standard counts only patches of 200 acres or more.","service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Fire/MapServer","layer":3,"where":"MostLikelyFireSeverity = ''High Severity'' AND ManagementZone <> ''''","x":"ManagementZone","xType":"category","xLabel":"Management zone","y":"Acres","stat":"sum","yName":"High severity acres","yLabel":"Acres","type":"bar","orientation":"h","sort":"desc"}],"mapLayer":{"service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Datadownloader_PlanningandJurisdictions/MapServer","layer":26,"title":"Forest management zones","unit":"zone","unitPlural":"Zones","name":"Name","countAsSites":false,"colorBy":"Name","colorByLabel":"Zone","meta":[{"field":"Acres","suffix":" acres"}],"fields":[{"field":"Name","label":"Zone"},{"field":"Acres","label":"Acres"}],"colors":{"WUI Defense Zone":"#e69800","WUI Threat Zone":"#f5ca7a","General Forest":"#abcd66","Desolation Wilderness":"#728944","Mt. Rose Wilderness":"#728944","Granite Chief Wilderness":"#728944"}}}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Landscape Fire Dynamics","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/192"},{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Wildland Urban Interface Defense Zone Fire Dynamics","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/193"}]');

For most of the last century the Basin saw few large fires, a result of fire suppression rather than of a forest that does not burn. The 2007 Angora Fire burned about 3,100 acres and 254 homes near South Lake Tahoe. The 2021 Caldor Fire burned about 9,900 acres inside the Basin, the largest fire in the record here, before firefighters stopped it in treated forest near Meyers.', N'Fire perimeters come from CAL FIRE''s statewide fire history, which maps fires on the California side of the Basin; Nevada fires and many small fires are not in it. Each perimeter is clipped to the TRPA boundary and measured in California Albers, so a fire that started outside the Basin counts only the acres that burned inside it.

The Tahoe Fire and Fuels Team fire history, 1988 to 2025, covers both states and will replace this record once it is published.', 0, N'map', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Acres burned in the Basin by year","sub":"Acres inside the TRPA boundary within mapped fire perimeters, California side.","json":"data/fire-history/area-burned-by-year.json","sourceHref":"https://services1.arcgis.com/jUJYIo9tSA7EHvfZ/arcgis/rest/services/California_Historic_Fire_Perimeters/FeatureServer/0","sourceLabel":"CAL FIRE California Historic Fire Perimeters, clipped to the TRPA boundary","x":"Year","xLabel":"Year","y":"Acres","yName":"Acres burned","yLabel":"Acres","type":"bar","span":"span-12"},{"title":"Largest fires in the Basin","sub":"Acres each fire burned inside the TRPA boundary, the 12 largest in the record.","json":"data/fire-history/largest-fires.json","sourceHref":"https://services1.arcgis.com/jUJYIo9tSA7EHvfZ/arcgis/rest/services/California_Historic_Fire_Perimeters/FeatureServer/0","sourceLabel":"CAL FIRE California Historic Fire Perimeters, clipped to the TRPA boundary","x":"Fire","xType":"category","xLabel":"Fire","y":"Acres","yName":"Acres in the Basin","yLabel":"Acres","type":"bar","orientation":"h","sort":"desc","span":"span-12"}],"mapLayer":{"url":"data/fire-history/fire-perimeters.geojson","sourceHref":"https://services1.arcgis.com/jUJYIo9tSA7EHvfZ/arcgis/rest/services/California_Historic_Fire_Perimeters/FeatureServer/0","sourceLabel":"CAL FIRE California Historic Fire Perimeters, clipped to the TRPA boundary","layer":0,"title":"Fire perimeters in the Basin","unit":"fire","unitPlural":"Fires","name":"Fire","countAsSites":false,"colorBy":"Year","colorByLabel":"Year","breaks":[{"max":1949,"label":"Before 1950","color":"var(--mon-turbid-2)"},{"max":1989,"label":"1950 to 1989","color":"var(--mon-turbid-3)"},{"max":2009,"label":"1990 to 2009","color":"var(--mon-turbid-4)"},{"max":2019,"label":"2010 to 2019","color":"var(--mon-turbid-5)"},{"max":9999,"label":"2020 and later","color