• Yellow pine forest: Jeffrey pine, white fir, incense cedar, sugar pine

• Red fir forest: red fir, Jeffrey pine, lodgepole pine, western white pine, mountain hemlock, western juniper

• Subalpine forest: whitebark pine, mountain hemlock, mountain mahogany

• Shrub association: greenleaf and pinemat manzanita, tobacco brush, Sierra chinquapin, huckleberry oak, mountain whitethorn

• Sagebrush scrub vegetation: Region sagebrush, bitterbrush, Douglas chaenactis

• Deciduous riparian: quaking aspen, mountain alder, black cottonwood, willow

• Meadow associations (wet and dry meadow): mountain squirrel tail, alpine gentian, whorled penstemon, asters, fescues, mountain brome, corn lilies, mountain bentgrass, hairgrass, marsh marigold, elephant heads, tinker''s penney, mountain timothy, sedges, rushes, buttercups

• Wetland associations (marsh vegetation): pond lilies, buckbean, mare''s tail, pondweed, common bladderwort, bottle sedge, common spikerush

• Cushion plant association (alpine scrub): alpine phlox, dwarf ragwort, draba', N'The map of dominate vegetation types in the Region was last updated in 2009. Since then only the Angora fire burn area has been updated. In 2009, satellite imagery, aerial photographs and field reconnaissance (USFS Forest Inventory and Analysis data) were used to delineate and classify vegetation types in the Lake Tahoe Region. This information is digitized into a geographic information system and subsequently analysed to summarize vegetation community richness. Information from the Tahoe Fire and Fuels Team (a multi-agency partnership) on forest fuels treatments and disturbance events are incorporated for year to year change in vegetative composition.', 0, N'charts', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Undisturbed acres by vegetation community","sub":"Acres of each vegetation community outside developed land, from the 2010 vegetation map.","layer":91,"where":"Development = ''Undeveloped'' AND TRPA_VegType <> ''''","x":"TRPA_VegType","xType":"category","xLabel":"Vegetation community","y":"SUM_Acres","stat":"sum","yName":"Acres","yLabel":"Acres","type":"bar","orientation":"h","sort":"desc","span":"span-12"},{"title":"Habitat types within each community","sub":"Undisturbed acres of each California Wildlife Habitat Relationships type within each community (2010).","layer":91,"where":"Development = ''Undeveloped'' AND TRPA_VegType <> ''''","x":"TRPA_VegType","xType":"category","xLabel":"Vegetation community","y":"SUM_Acres","stat":"sum","seriesBy":"WHRTYPE","seriesLabel":"Habitat type","seriesNames":{"ADS":"Alpine dwarf shrub","MCP":"Montane chaparral","ASP":"Aspen","MHC":"Montane hardwood-conifer","MRI":"Montane riparian","PGS":"Perennial grassland","WTM":"Wet meadow","EPN":"Eastside pine","JPN":"Jeffrey pine","LPN":"Lodgepole pine","SMC":"Sierran mixed conifer","WFR":"White fir","RFR":"Red fir","JUN":"Juniper","SCN":"Subalpine conifer","BAR":"Barren","BBR":"Bitterbrush","LSG":"Low sage","SGB":"Sagebrush","LAC":"Lacustrine"},"yLabel":"Acres","type":"stacked","orientation":"h","span":"span-12"}]}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Vegetation Community Richness","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/74"}]');

Stream environment zones (SEZ) play a variety of critical roles in the Region including natural water filtration, storage, and conveyance of surface runoff (Roby et al., 2015). Development encroaching on these areas reduces their potential to filter sediment and nutrients, and also reduces the amount of surface runoff they can effectively treat. Naturally functioning SEZs also provide open space, flood flow capacity, riparian vegetation, and fish and wildlife habitat, and buffer urban uses in developed areas. SEZ protection and restoration contributes to achievement of other environmental threshold standards, including water quality, wildlife, fisheries, vegetation preservation, recreation, and scenic resources. Even seemingly unrelated threshold standards such as air quality and noise are affected by SEZs. For instance, aspen stands in SEZs next to roadways have been shown to help physically block air particulates from spreading to adjacent areas and moderate roadway noise.

Threshold standard: A non-degradation standard shall apply to native deciduous trees, wetlands, and meadows to preserve plant communities and significant wildlife habitat, while providing for opportunities to increase the acreage of such riparian associations to be consistent with the SEZ threshold.', N'Whether or not the TRPA Goals and Policies continue to support the non-degradation of SEZs.', 0, N'map', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Riparian, meadow, and wetland acres by condition","sub":"Undisturbed and disturbed acres of each community (2010).","layer":91,"where":"TRPA_VegType IN (''Deciduous Riparian'',''Meadow'',''Wetland'')","x":"TRPA_VegType","xType":"category","xLabel":"Community","y":"SUM_Acres","stat":"sum","seriesBy":"Development","seriesLabel":"Condition","yLabel":"Acres","type":"stacked"},{"title":"SEZ acres restored by project year","sub":"Acres of stream environment zone restored by completed projects, by year.","layer":77,"x":"YearCompleted","y":"AcresRestored","stat":"sum","yName":"Acres restored","yLabel":"Acres","type":"bar"}],"mapLayer":{"service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/SEZ_Viewer_Base_Data/MapServer","layer":7,"title":"Riparian areas","unit":"riparian area","unitPlural":"Riparian areas","name":"Class_name","countAsSites":false,"meta":[{"field":"Acres","suffix":" acres"}],"fields":[{"field":"Acres","label":"Acres"}]},"overlays":[{"service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/DataDownloader_SoilsandHydro/MapServer","layer":42,"title":"Aspen stands (USFS)","name":"Acres","geometry":"fill","color":"var(--mon-stevens4-4)","fields":[{"field":"Acres","label":"Acres"}]},{"service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/DataDownloader_SoilsandHydro/MapServer","layer":40,"title":"Meadows (UC Davis and USFS)","name":"ID","geometry":"fill","color":"var(--mon-stevens4-2)","fields":[{"field":"AREA_ACRE","label":"Acres"}]},{"service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/DataDownloader_SoilsandHydro/MapServer","layer":41,"title":"Fens (USFS)","name":"LOCATION_N","geometry":"fill","color":"var(--mon-stevens4-1)","fields":[{"field":"PERC_FEN","label":"Percent fen"},{"field":"DESCRIPTIO","label":"Description"}]}]}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Non-Degradation of Stream Environment Zones","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/82"}]');

Vegetation is integral to many scenic, wildlife, and recreational amenities in the Lake Tahoe Region. Vegetation also provides functional services including soil stabilization, nutrient cycling, surface water flow regulation, air purification, and noise control. The focus of vegetation preservation in the Region is to protect and maintain these and other attributes.

Threshold standard: Native vegetation shall be maintained at a maximum level to be consistent with the limits defined in the Land Capability Classification of the Lake Tahoe Region, California-Nevada, A Guide for Planning (Bailey, 1974), for allowable impervious cover and permanent site disturbance. The report can be found on the TRPA website at: https://www.trpa.gov/wp-content/uploads/documents/archive/Bailey-Land-Capability-Report.pdf.', N'Are TRPA policies in place to conform to the adopted standard?', 0, N'charts', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Land capability district acres","sub":"Acres in each land capability district (NRCS 2007 mapping).","service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Soils_and_Land_Capability/MapServer","layer":6,"where":"Land_Capab <> '' ''","x":"Land_Capab","xType":"category","xLabel":"Land capability district","y":"GISAcre","stat":"sum","yName":"Acres","yLabel":"Acres","type":"bar"},{"title":"Impervious cover by land capability district, 2019","sub":"Acres of impervious surface in each district, by surface type.","layer":81,"where":"Feature IS NOT NULL AND Land_Capab IS NOT NULL","x":"Land_Capab","xType":"category","xLabel":"Land capability district","y":"GISAcre","stat":"sum","seriesBy":"Feature","seriesLabel":"Surface","yLabel":"Acres","type":"stacked"},{"title":"Coverage allowed under Bailey (1974) coefficients","sub":"District acres times the Bailey coverage coefficient for each district.","layer":81,"where":"Land_Capab IS NOT NULL","x":"Land_Capab","xType":"category","xLabel":"Land capability district","y":"GISAcre * Bailey_Co","stat":"sum","yName":"Allowed acres","yLabel":"Acres","type":"bar"}]}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Consistency with Bailey Land Capability System","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/83"}]');

CSCI stream score greater than 0.92 = excellent health

CSCI stream score of 0.79 to 0.92 = good health

CSCI stream score of less than 0.79 = degraded health', N'TRPA''s stream monitoring program uses the Surface Water Ambient Monitoring Program bioassessment protocol, which measures Benthic Macroinvertebrate (BMI) composition and physical stream habitat to assess overall stream health. BMI composition and habitat is compared against pristine streams using the California Stream Condition Index to determine the biotic integrity of streams. Probabilistic and targeted sampling is used to assess the overall health of Tahoe streams, changes in stream health over time, and to assess large scale restoration and BMP implementation projects. 20 probabilistic, one-time "status" sites are sampled per year, as well as 73 "trend" sites re-visited every four years. Only trend sites are displayed on this page. Status site information can be found at EcoAtlas.org

To download all of the stream data on this page please see Tahoe Open Data.', 1, N'map', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Average condition score at trend sites","sub":"Average California Stream Condition Index (CSCI) score at trend sites, by sampling period.","layer":84,"x":"Year","xType":"category","xLabel":"Period","y":"Value","yLabel":"CSCI score","type":"bar","seriesBy":"Trend_Panel","seriesLabel":"Sites","seriesNames":{"Both":"All trend sites","A":"Panel A","B":"Panel B"}},{"title":"Streams by condition class","sub":"Share of sampled streams in each CSCI condition class, by sampling period.","layer":83,"x":"Year","xType":"category","xLabel":"Period","y":"Value","yLabel":"Share of streams","format":".0%","type":"stacked","seriesBy":"Condition_Class","seriesLabel":"Condition class"},{"title":"Average stream condition score","sub":"Average California Stream Condition Index (CSCI) score of sites sampled each year. Choose a site type.","layer":8,"x":"YEAR_OF_COUNT","y":"COUNT_VALUE","stat":"avg","yName":"Average CSCI score","yLabel":"CSCI score","pick":"STATION_TYPE","pickLabel":"Site type","type":"bar"},{"title":"Sites sampled each year","sub":"Bioassessment sites sampled each year, by site type.","layer":8,"x":"YEAR_OF_COUNT","y":"OBJECTID","stat":"count","yLabel":"Sites sampled","seriesBy":"STATION_TYPE","seriesLabel":"Site type","type":"stacked"},{"title":"Condition score by site","sub":"CSCI score each year the site was sampled, for trend and reference sites. Choose a site.","layer":8,"where":"STATION_TYPE IN (''Trend'', ''Trend Panel A'', ''Trend Panel B'', ''Reference'')","x":"YEAR_OF_COUNT","y":"COUNT_VALUE","yName":"CSCI score","yLabel":"CSCI score","pick":"SITE_NAME","pickLabel":"Site","span":"span-12"}],"sitesLayer":{"layer":8,"name":"SITE_NAME","year":"YEAR_OF_COUNT","status":"STATION_TYPE"},"sitesPerYear":{"layer":8,"year":"YEAR_OF_COUNT","site":"SITE_NAME"},"siteProfile":{"record":{"year":"YEAR_OF_COUNT","value":"COUNT_VALUE","label":"CSCI score","decimals":2,"noun":["sample","samples"]},"facts":[{"label":"Duration","field":"DURATION"},{"label":"Station code","field":"STATION_CODE"}],"link":"LTINFO"}}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Stream Habitat Condition","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/173"}]');

There are 63 tributaries that drain into Lake Tahoe, and one tributary that drains from the basin. The amount of water flowing through a stream is primarily dependent on the size of its watershed and the amount of precipitation within a given year. Streams provide critical habitat to a diversity of native and non-native fish populations and other riverine-dependent organisms. Historic logging, grazing, and land use interrupted the hydrologic integrity of many of the streams and tributaries draining into Lake Tahoe, and the results of these legacy activities are evident today. Alteration of stream flow regimes, such as water diversions, can result in adverse impacts to stream habitat diversity, function, and productivity of aquatic ecosystems and organisms. A component of the TRPA Regional Plan is to maintain a healthy functioning fishery through the conservation and restoration of natural flow regimes.

Threshold standard: Until instream flow standards are established in the Regional Plan to protect fishery values, a non-degradation standard shall apply to instream flows.', N'Three criteria were evaluated to determine the implementation status of the instream flow threshold standards:

1. Has TRPA adopted appropriate policies, ordinances and programs to support the adopted threshold standards?

2. Has TRPA permitted or otherwise allowed for new permanent diversions or alteration of stream flows since 1987?

3. Does available scientific information support the need to adopt instream flow standards for Regional streams?', 0, N'context', NULL, N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Non-Degradation Standard for Instream Flow","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/21"}]');

Divert Stream Intakes to Lake Sources is include in the Threshold Dashboard. Threshold Indicators are evaluated against Threshold Standards every 4 years. Thresholds are environmental goals and standards for the Lake Tahoe Basin that indirectly define the capacity of the Region to accommodate additional land development.

There are 63 tributaries that drain into Lake Tahoe, and one tributary that drains from the basin. The amount of water flowing through a stream is primarily dependent on the size of its watershed and the amount of precipitation within a given year. Streams provide critical habitat to a diversity of native and non-native fish populations and other riverine dependent organisms. Historic logging, grazing and land uses interrupted the hydrologic integrity of many of the streams and tributaries draining into Lake Tahoe, and the results of these legacy activities are evident today. Alteration of stream flow regimes, such as water diversions, can result in adverse impacts to stream habitat diversity, function, and productivity of aquatic ecosystems and organisms. A component of the TRPA Regional Plan is to maintain a healthy functioning fishery through the conservation and restoration of natural flow regimes.

Threshold standard: It shall be a policy of the TRPA Governing Board to seek transfers of existing points of water diversion from streams to Lake Tahoe.', N'Evidence of TRPA support for Management Standard.', 0, N'map', N'{"sitesLayer":{"service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Shoreline_Resources/MapServer","layer":4,"name":"SITE_NAME","code":"SID","status":"STATUS","unit":"intake"}}', NULL);

Provide disturbance zones and influence zones for Bald Eagle (Winter).', N'Professional and volunteer biologists stationed at a series of observation points surrounding Lake Tahoe record all observed eagles over the same four-hour period once a year following protocols developed by the National Wildlife Federation.', 0, N'charts', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Wintering bald eagles","sub":"Bald eagles counted in the annual winter survey, with the threshold of 2.","layer":96,"where":"Wildlife_Species = ''Bald Eagle - winter''","x":"Year","y":"Total","yName":"Individuals","yLabel":"Individuals","type":"bar","span":"span-12","standard":{"value":2}}],"mapLayer":{"service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Wildlife_Habitat/MapServer","layer":1,"title":"Bald eagle winter habitat","unit":"habitat area","unitPlural":"Habitat areas","name":"WINTER_HABITAT","countAsSites":false,"fields":[{"field":"WINTER_HABITAT","label":"Area"}]}}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Wintering Bald Eagle Population Sites","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/159"}]');

Provide disturbance zones and influence zones for Golden Eagle.', N'Since 2010 there have been no formal surveys as a result of cutbacks to the U.S. Forest Service wildlife monitoring program.', 0, N'map', N'{"mapLayer":{"service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Wildlife_Activity/MapServer","layer":1,"title":"Golden eagle nest buffers","unit":"nest buffer","unitPlural":"Nest buffers","name":"Species","list":false,"countAsSites":false,"fields":[{"field":"Buffer_Distance","label":"Buffer (m)"}]}}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Golden Eagle Population Sites","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/161"}]');

Disturbance-free zones protect important sites that are used by special interest species by restricting habitat manipulations or other activities that would degrade the condition of habitat within the site. The zones are areas where activities that would disturb the species are minimized.

The disturbance-free zones include a 0.5-mile radius buffer around bald eagle nests and a 0.25-mile radius buffer around osprey, golden eagle, and peregrine falcon nests. Goshawk disturbance-free zones include both a 0.25-mile circular buffer and the most suitable 500 acres of habitat surrounding the nest site. Specific areas are mapped for wintering bald eagles, deer, and waterfowl. Influence zones include a 3.5-mile radius buffer for goshawk, a 0.6-mile radius for osprey, a 9.0-mile radius buffer for golden eagle, and a 7.6-mile radius buffer for peregrine falcon. Specific influence areas are mapped for wintering bald eagle, deer, and waterfowl, and a variable influence zone is set for bald eagle nests.

Northern goshawks and bald eagles are also considered sensitive species by the USDA Forest Service, and they receive additional consideration in all management decisions affecting Forest Service lands.

Threshold standard: Provide disturbance zones in meadows and influence zones in mapped areas for Deer.', N'The TRPA Goals and Policies supports the maintenance of disturbance-free zones.', 0, N'map', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Fawning habitat by vegetation type","sub":"Acres of mapped deer fawning habitat (1996), the disturbance-free zone, by vegetation type.","layer":45,"x":"WHRTYPE","xType":"category","xLabel":"Vegetation type","xNames":{"KMC":"Klamath mixed conifer","JPN":"Jeffrey pine","WFR":"White fir","LPN":"Lodgepole pine","RFR":"Red fir","SCN":"Subalpine conifer"},"y":"ACRES","stat":"sum","yName":"Acres","yLabel":"Acres","type":"bar","orientation":"h","sort":"desc"}],"mapLayer":{"layer":45,"title":"Deer fawning habitat","unit":"habitat area","unitPlural":"Habitat areas","name":"WHRTYPE","list":false,"countAsSites":false,"fields":[{"field":"WHRTYPE","label":"Habitat type"},{"field":"ACRES","label":"Acres"},{"field":"SUITBILTY","label":"Suitability"}]}}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Deer Disturbance-Free Zone","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/165"}]');

This standard is encompassed by the Non-Degradation standard, https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/ThresholdIndicator/Detail/82, that applies to native deciduous trees, wetlands, and meadows to preserve plan communities and significant wildlife habitat.

Riparian Habitat is include in the Threshold Dashboard. Threshold Indicators are evaluated against Threshold Standards every 4 years. Thresholds are environmental goals and standards for the Lake Tahoe Basin that indirectly define the capacity of the Region to accommodate additional land development.

Riparian and wetland areas contain a mosaic of vegetation communities, moisture gradients, and microclimates that make them some of the most diverse and productive terrestrial areas for wildlife (Kondolf et al. 1996). Riparian and wetland areas occupy a small percentage of lands within the basin, but they support more wildlife species than any other vegetation types (Kattleman and Embury, 1996). For example, in an area with similar environmental conditions in the nearby Inyo National Forest, riparian and wetland areas occupied less than one percent of the land area but were important to 75 percent of the local wildlife species (Kondolf et al. 1987). The same areas are desirable for human-related uses, which have resulted in the degradation of 75 percent of all marshes and 50 percent of all meadows within the basin (Kattleman, R and Embury, M 1996)(Manley et al. 2000). Since riparian and wetland areas are relatively rare and important compared to other vegetation types, even small modifications to these areas can have a greater impact on wildlife species than modifications in other areas (Lindstrom, Rucks, and Wigand 2000; Elliot-Fisk et al. 1996).

Threshold standard: A nondegradation standard shall apply to significant wildlife habitat consisting of deciduous trees, wetlands, and meadows while providing for opportunities to increase the acreage of such riparian associations.', N'Whether TRPA Goals and Policies continue to support the protection of “wildlife habitat consisting of deciduous trees, wetlands, and meadows.”', 0, N'context', N'{"mapLayer":{"service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/SEZ_Viewer_Base_Data/MapServer","layer":7,"title":"Riparian areas","unit":"riparian area","unitPlural":"Riparian areas","name":"Class_name","countAsSites":false,"meta":[{"field":"Acres","suffix":" acres"}],"fields":[{"field":"Acres","label":"Acres"}]}}', NULL);

In 2020, 72 percent of the Region''s residents lived in the California portion of the Basin and 28 percent in Nevada, and 64 percent lived in south shore communities. California communities declined 2 percent from 2010 to 2020 and are 12 percent below the 2000 peak, while Nevada communities grew 7 percent.

Incline Village and Crystal Bay grew fastest from 2010 to 2020, up 8 percent. Homewood fell 9 percent and Tahoma 7 percent. The City of South Lake Tahoe declined 2 percent, and Meyers grew 3 percent. The Placer County communities on the north and west shores declined 4 percent from 2010 and are 17 percent below the 2000 peak; only Lake Forest and Dollar Hill grew, up 3 percent.', N'Figures come from the U.S. Census Bureau''s decennial census and American Community Survey (ACS) 5-year estimates, queried through the Census Bureau''s API and summarized for the Tahoe Region by TRPA. Decennial counts are a full count every 10 years; ACS estimates are yearly averages of five years of samples, so they move more smoothly and carry a margin of error.

In 2020 the census boundaries changed. The Lake Tahoe Region is made up of 31 census tracts and 78 block groups along the California and Nevada border. The Carson City tract straddles the TRPA boundary with most of its people outside it, so it is left out.

The tables and boundaries are on the TRPA Demographics map service, and the code behind the figures is in TRPA''s Demographics repository on GitHub.', 0, N'map', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Population at each census","sub":"Residents of the Lake Tahoe Region counted in each decennial census.","service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Demographics/MapServer","layer":18,"where":"Geography = ''Basin'' AND Category = ''Total Population'' AND Source LIKE ''%Decennial%''","x":"Year","y":"Value","yName":"Residents","yLabel":"Residents","type":"bar"},{"title":"Population estimates between censuses","sub":"American Community Survey 5-year estimates of the Region''s population.","service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Demographics/MapServer","layer":18,"where":"Geography = ''Basin'' AND Category = ''Total Population'' AND (Source = ''acs/acs5'' OR Source LIKE ''%ACS%'')","x":"Year","y":"Value","yName":"Residents","yLabel":"Residents","zero":false},{"title":"Population change by community, 2010 to 2020","sub":"Change in each community''s population between the 2010 and 2020 censuses.","service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Demographics/MapServer","layer":31,"where":"1=1","x":"NEIGHBORHOOD","y":"Population_Percent_Change","xType":"category","xLabel":"Community","yName":"Change","yLabel":"Change (percent)","type":"bar","orientation":"h","sort":"desc","span":"span-12"}],"mapLayer":{"service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/Demographics/MapServer","layer":31,"title":"Population change, 2010 to 2020","unit":"community","unitPlural":"Communities","name":"NEIGHBORHOOD","countAsSites":false,"colorBy":"Population_Percent_Change","colorByLabel":"Population change","breaks":[{"max":-5,"label":"Down 5 to 15 percent","color":"var(--mon-chlorophyll-2)"},{"max":0,"label":"Down less than 5 percent","color":"var(--mon-chlorophyll-3)"},{"max":5,"label":"Up less than 5 percent","color":"var(--mon-chlorophyll-5)"},{"max":15,"label":"Up 5 to 15 percent","color":"var(--mon-chlorophyll-6)"}],"meta":[{"field":"Population_Percent_Change","suffix":" percent"}],"fields":[{"field":"Neighborhood_Population_2010","label":"Population, 2010"},{"field":"Neighborhood_Population_2020","label":"Population, 2020"},{"field":"Population_Percent_Change","label":"Population change (percent)"},{"field":"Neighborhood_HousingUnits_2020","label":"Housing units, 2020"},{"field":"Neighborhood_Seasonal_2020","label":"Seasonal homes, 2020"},{"field":"Seasonal_Unit_Percent_Change","label":"Seasonal homes change (percent)"}]}}', NULL);

Millennials form a second peak, at ages 25 to 40, that the surrounding five counties do not show, perhaps because of the Region''s appeal to people seeking an active outdoor life. One resident in five was 65 or older in 2020, up from fewer than one in 10 in 1990.', N'Figures come from the U.S. Census Bureau''s decennial census and American Community Survey (ACS) 5-year estimates, queried through the Census Bureau''s API and summarized for the Tahoe Region by TRPA. Decennial counts are a full count every 10 years; ACS estimates are yearly averages of five years of samples, so they move more smoothly and carry a margin of error.

In 2020 the census boundaries changed. The Lake Tahoe Region is made up of 31 census tracts and 78 block groups along the California and Nevada border. The Carson City tract straddles the TRPA boundary with most of its people outside it, so it is left out.

