Restrictions come in three income levels: affordable, for households earning up to 80 percent of area median income; moderate, up to 120 percent; and achievable, for local workers at any income.', N'TRPA records each deed restriction with its parcel, type, and number of units, and maps the parcels that carry one.', 0, N'map', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Deed-restricted units by type","sub":"Housing units with a recorded deed restriction, by income level.","service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/LTinfo_Climate_Resilience_Dashboard/MapServer","layer":148,"x":"Deed_Restriction_Type","xType":"category","xLabel":"Restriction","xOrder":["Affordable","Moderate","Achievable"],"y":"Units","stat":"sum","yName":"Units","yLabel":"Units","type":"bar"}],"mapLayer":{"service":"https://maps.trpa.org/server/rest/services/LTinfo_Climate_Resilience_Dashboard/MapServer","layer":20,"title":"Deed-restricted parcels","unit":"parcel","unitPlural":"Parcels","name":"APO_ADDRESS","countAsSites":false,"where":"DeedRestrictionStatus = ''Recorded''","colorBy":"DeedRestrictionType","colorByLabel":"Restriction","colors":{"Affordable Housing":"var(--mon-stevens4-1)","Moderate Income Housing":"var(--mon-stevens4-2)","Achievable Housing":"var(--mon-stevens4-3)"},"labels":{"Affordable Housing":"Affordable","Moderate Income Housing":"Moderate","Achievable Housing":"Achievable"},"fields":[{"field":"APN","label":"APN"},{"field":"DeedRestrictionType","label":"Restriction"},{"field":"RecordingDate","label":"Recorded"},{"field":"JURISDICTION","label":"Jurisdiction"}]}}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Climate Resilience Dashboard","Label":"Access to Housing","Url":"https://climate.laketahoeinfo.org/access-to-housing"}]');

Maintaining the biological integrity of Lake Tahoe’s nearshore environment is a longtime goal of resource managers in the region and has been set forth in a variety of planning documents. Submerged aquatic vegetation is an important biological component within Lake Tahoe’s nearshore context. Knowing the status of submerged aquatic plant populations at Lake Tahoe has become increasingly important to nearshore managers due to the introduction and spread of invasive species such as Eurasian watermilfoil (Myriophyllum spicatum) and curly-leaf pondweed (Potamogeton crispus). Aquatic invasive plants when left unchecked can impact water quality, fish and wildlife habitat conditions, recreational fishing, human and animal health, vessel navigation, and the recreation values of water bodies.

The threshold standard establishes a goal for aquatic invasive plant infestations in Lake Tahoe and associated areas. The standard establishes the goal that all aquatic invasive plant infestations in Lake Tahoe (not including the Tahoe Keys, which has a separate standard) be in the surveillance category. The goal aligns with the management categories that are utilized by the Lake Tahoe Aquatic Invasive Species Coordinating Committee and conveys the reality of long-term management of aquatic invasive species. Due to aquatic invasive plants’ proclivity towards spreading and establishing new infestations through fragmentation throughout connected waterbodies, all completely treated AIP sites are at risk of re-establishment if there is source of AIP fragments within Lake Tahoe. The goal acknowledges that because of this risk, complete eradication is unlikely, and control will likely require continued surveillance and vigilance. The threshold standard formally adopts the phase one vision laid out in the Lake Tahoe Region AIS Action Agenda.

Threshold standard: No active aquatic invasive plant infestations in Lake Tahoe, adjacent wetlands, and tributaries, not including the Tahoe Keys.', N'The standard establishes the goal that all aquatic invasive plant infestations in the Lake be in the surveillance category.', 0, N'charts', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Aquatic invasive plant sites by status","sub":"Percent of known infestation sites in each management status, 2023 Threshold Evaluation.","json":"data/threshold-evaluation/ais-site-status.json","sourceHref":"https://trpa-agency.github.io/ThresholdEvaluation/2023/WaterQuality/Chart/Final/AIS_Infestation.html","sourceLabel":"2023 Threshold Evaluation","x":"Year","xLabel":"Year","y":"Percent","seriesBy":"Status","seriesLabel":"Status","seriesOrder":["Planning","Control","Surveillance"],"yLabel":"Percent of sites","type":"stacked","span":"span-12"}]}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"No Active Aquatic Invasive Plant Infestations","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/186"},{"Dashboard":"Climate Resilience Dashboard","Label":"Aquatic Invasive Species","Url":"https://climate.laketahoeinfo.org/aquatic-invasive-species"}]');

Maintaining the biological integrity of Lake Tahoe’s nearshore environment is a longtime goal of resource managers in the region and has been set forth in a variety of planning documents. Submerged aquatic vegetation is an important biological component within Lake Tahoe’s nearshore context.

The Tahoe Keys is the largest and most persistent population of aquatic weeds growing in the Region, infesting over 172 acres of waterways. Due to aquatic invasive plants’ proclivity to spread and establish new infestations through fragmentation throughout connected waterbodies, source fragements from the Tahoe Keys are a threat to the rest of Lake Tahoe.

Two aquatic invasive plants, Eurasian watermilfoil (Myriophyllum spicatum) and curly-leaf pondweed (Potamogeton crispus), are the primary targets of the threshold standard. Aquatic invasive plants, when left unchecked, can impact water quality, fish and wildlife habitat conditions, recreational fishing, human and animal health, vessel navigation, and the recreation values of water bodies.

The goal aligns with the management categories that are utilized by the Lake Tahoe Aquatic Invasive Species Coordinating Committee and conveys the reality of long-term management of aquatic invasive species.

Threshold standard: Reduce average aquatic invasive plant abundance in the Tahoe Keys by a minimum of 75 percent from the 2020 baseline year.', N'Reduce average aquatic invasive plant abundance in the Tahoe Keys by a minimum of 75 percent from the 2020 baseline year.', 0, N'charts', N'{"measures":[{"title":"Aquatic invasive plant volume in the Tahoe Keys","sub":"Cubic yards of each invasive plant in the Tahoe Keys lagoons, 2023 Threshold Evaluation.","json":"data/threshold-evaluation/tahoe-keys-aquatic-plants.json","sourceHref":"https://trpa-agency.github.io/ThresholdEvaluation/2023/WaterQuality/Chart/Final/Tahoe_Keys_Infestation.html","sourceLabel":"2023 Threshold Evaluation","x":"Year","xType":"category","xLabel":"Year","y":"Cubic yards","seriesBy":"Species","seriesLabel":"Species","yLabel":"Cubic yards","type":"bar","span":"span-12"}]}', N'[{"Dashboard":"Threshold Dashboard","Label":"Reduce Average Aquatic Invasive Plant Abundance in the Tahoe Keys","Url":"https://thresholds.laketahoeinfo.org/threshold/indicators/187"},{"Dashboard":"Climate Resilience Dashboard","Label":"Aquatic Invasive Species","Url":"https://climate.laketahoeinfo.org/aquatic-invasive-species"}]');

